lessons.example:
Searching for 9 in `[1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11]`:

| lo | hi | mid | a[mid] | decision |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 5 | 2 | 5 | 5 < 9 → lo = 3 |
| 3 | 5 | 4 | 9 | found at index 4 |

And searching for 4 (absent):

| lo | hi | mid | a[mid] | decision |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 5 | 2 | 5 | 5 > 4 → hi = 1 |
| 0 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 < 4 → lo = 1 |
| 1 | 1 | 1 | 3 | 3 < 4 → lo = 2 |
| 2 | 1 | — | — | lo > hi: not found, return −1 |

The same loop in C++:

```cpp
int lo = 0, hi = n - 1;
while (lo <= hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == target) return mid;
    if (a[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
    else hi = mid - 1;
}
return -1;
```

Searching for 0 in `[4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2]`:

| lo | hi | mid | a[mid] | sorted half | target inside it? | next |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 6 | 3 | 7 | left: 4..7 | no | lo = 4 |
| 4 | 6 | 5 | 1 | left: 0..1 | yes (0 <= 0 < 1) | hi = 4 |
| 4 | 4 | 4 | 0 | — | found | index 4 |

